{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 153 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "be6a60bb7f54173c17276a97aa0e7c5307b3fc8a82be73ae2b754bd65519715e" :edb/db-before-t 152 :edb/db-after-t 153 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533465 50 #inst "2026-10-10T23:33:12.963Z" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186059704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394337 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186059741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394434 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186059785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394475 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186059786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394482 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186059786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394489 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186059819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394132 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059058 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186060411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394009 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060411 1071 "Consider the set $X = {3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24}$. Which subset must be placed into the blank in the set below so that it represents a partition of $X$?\n\n${{6, 12, 18}, {15, 21}, ____}$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186060411 1071 "Consider the set $X={3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24}.$ Which subset must be placed into the blank in the set below so that it represents a partition of $X?$\n\n$$\n{{6,12,18},{15,21},\\,____\\,}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060411 1074 "A partition of a set $X$ is a collection of non-empty subsets of $X$ where every element of $X$ is contained in *exactly* one subset. \n\nTo find the subset that goes in the blank, we must identify which elements of $X$ are not yet contained within any of the given subsets.\n\nLet's go through each element of $X$ and check:\n\n- $3$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\n- $6\\in {6,12,18}\\,✓$\n\n- $9$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\n- $12\\in {6,12,18}\\,✓$\n\n- $15\\in {15,21}\\,✓$\n\n- $18\\in {6,12,18}\\,✓$\n\n- $21\\in {15,21}\\,✓$\n\n- $24$ does not belong to any of the given subsets $\\,\\times $\n\nThe elements $3,$ $9,$ and $24$ are not yet contained within any of the given subsets. Therefore, the subset that must go in the blank is ${3,9,24}.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059082 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186060415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394325 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060415 1074 "De Morgan's laws state that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(A∧B)≡¬A∨¬B, \\\\ ¬(A∨B)≡¬A∧¬B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the given statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬((A∧B)∧(A∨¬B)) & ≡¬(A∧B)∨¬(A∨¬B) \\\\  & ≡(¬A∨¬B)∨(¬A∧¬(¬B)) \\\\  & ≡(¬A∨¬B)∨(¬A∧B)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186059100 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186060418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394331 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060418 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(X∧Y)⇒Y$." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186060418 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(X∧Y)⇒Y.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186060418 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(X∧Y)⇒Y≡¬(¬(X∧Y))∨Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(X∧Y))∨Y≡(X∧Y)∨Y.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n(X∧Y)∨Y≡Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $Y.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186064292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394022 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186068785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395376 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186068820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395369 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186068820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395594 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186069548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186068428 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186069548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395588 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186069548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186069548 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y = \\frac{3}{2x}$?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186069548 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2x}?$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186069548 1074 "To plot $y=\\frac{3}{2x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/a3/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2x}.$\n\n![](images/5a/5a43656a35d47c7321e53d56238cd10f669bc807d57f72fcc002d79fb21aa111.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186073820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395429 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186083227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394626 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186083229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394647 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186083304 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393925 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186083317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394640 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186083318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394633 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186083399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394248 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394577 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393960 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394091 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394153 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393799 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393806 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393813 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393820 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393827 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395207 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394681 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394688 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087772 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394496 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087787 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393764 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186087803 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394695 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186088430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087203 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186088430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394571 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186088430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186088430 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $(y + 1)^{2} - ((x - 3)^{2})/(2) = 1$." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186088430 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $(y+1)^{2}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}=1.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186088430 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ (0+1)^{2}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $(3,0).$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394792 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393883 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393890 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393862 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393897 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393904 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393911 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092239 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394002 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394276 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394283 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394290 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394702 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092340 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394379 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092350 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394344 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092350 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394351 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092351 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394598 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394413 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394709 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091168 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394181 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092833 1071 "![](images/d1/d111b1df75cfaa0d86d17c06584c57b19ca4711ae3e6720e68d1704fd72d86ae.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092833 1071 "![](images/d1/d111b1df75cfaa0d86d17c06584c57b19ca4711ae3e6720e68d1704fd72d86ae.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092833 1074 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/f1/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd0496914b35f969896c6a54907fbfa5ea6c139c878d8682b933a8757bde7524.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091186 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394407 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092836 1071 "![](images/42/4229e1e8c92803a4d51043442a8bc65a6560e11a1e7559af7ec4246fe3005e75.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation that acts on vectors in $R^{2}$ as follows (in the order given):\n\n- it shears vectors by a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction, and then\n- rotates the result by $\\frac{π}{2}$ radians clockwise about the origin.\n\nWhat is the image of the unit square shown above under the action of the linear transformation?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092836 1071 "![](images/42/4229e1e8c92803a4d51043442a8bc65a6560e11a1e7559af7ec4246fe3005e75.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation that acts on vectors in ${R}^{2}$ as follows (in the order given):\n\n- it shears vectors by a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction, and then\n\n- rotates the result by $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians clockwise about the origin.\n\nWhat is the image of the unit square shown above under the action of the linear transformation?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092836 1074 "First, we apply the shear by a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction:\n\n![](images/c0/c0dfba56ed4c0ceb0b1739304c562040a2a69044117665bba5bf4fc2f506044f.png)\n\nThen, we apply the rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians clockwise:\n\n![](images/ff/ff3dca52efc6e985e500f22ce43658855c09901535378081ed6fc07d5bfec115.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091210 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394545 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092840 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $16x^{2} - y^{2} - 64x - 2y + 47 = 0$." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092840 1071 "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $16{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-64x-2y+47=0.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092840 1074 "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-64x-2y+47 & =0 \\\\ (16{x}^{2}-64x)+(-{y}^{2}-2y)+47 & =0 \\\\ 16[{x}^{2}-4x]-[{y}^{2}+2y]+47 & =0 \\\\ 16[(x-2)^{2}-4]-[(y+1)^{2}-1]+47 & =0 \\\\ 16(x-2)^{2}-64-(y+1)^{2}+1+47 & =0 \\\\ 16(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =64-1-47 \\\\ 16(x-2)^{2}-(y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{16(x-2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{16}{16} \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{1}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nh=2,\\,k=-1,\\,a=\\sqrt{1}=1,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{1}(x-2) \\\\  & =\\pm 4(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091510 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394799 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092890 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=5y \\\\ x=-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092890 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n${2x = 5y; x =-2$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092890 1074 "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-5y=0 \\\\ x=-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 0-(-5)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091834 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395256 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092944 1071 "If $k(1 - 3^{6})$ is the sum of the first $6$ terms of the geometric series $1 + 3 + 9 + ⋯$, then $k =$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186092944 1071 "If $k(1-{3}^{6})$ is the sum of the first $6$ terms of the geometric series $1+3+9+⋯,$ then $k=$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186092944 1074 "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nIn our case, ${a}_{1}=1.$ To find the value of $r,$ we find the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=1,N=6$ and $r=3$ into our formula, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{6} & =1\\cdot \\frac{1-{3}^{6}}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{3}^{6}}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(1-{3}^{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the above with $k(1-{3}^{6}),$ we obtain $k=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186096704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393939 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186096717 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394605 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186096768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394612 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186096769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394619 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186096844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395636 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186096844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395650 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186096845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395615 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186096845 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395643 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186096846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395692 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186100940 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393981 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186105773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394167 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395214 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395221 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186106496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186105094 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186106496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394057 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186106496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186106496 1071 "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v) = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, what is the standard matrix of $T$?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186106496 1071 "A linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186106496 1074 "First, we are told that $T$ represents a stretch along the $x$-axis or $y$-axis.\n\nComparing the $x$- and $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v),$ we note the following:\n\n- the $x$-component of $T(v)$ is $2$ times the $x$-component of $v,$ while\n\n- the $y$-components of $v$ and $T(v)$ are the same.\n\nSo, we conclude that $T$ represents a stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction. \n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186110664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394833 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186115235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393869 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186115315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393995 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186115415 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393743