w$." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186134527 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186134527 1074 "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{10\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 1+14\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{10\\cdot 2+6\\cdot (-2)+14\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}w=3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393932 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394976 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393946 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147496 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395349 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147496 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395356 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395527 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147497 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395534 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395402 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395409 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395416 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395541 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395548 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395555 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395562 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395569 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186147624 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394873 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147068 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186148224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395134 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148224 1071 "![](images/cb/cbccf40aca753c4c23cc2b159697deca70b45844d2cbf458c9a04f6dc6d3026f.png)\n\nThe phase line of a differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the slope field of the equation?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148224 1071 "![](images/cb/cbccf40aca753c4c23cc2b159697deca70b45844d2cbf458c9a04f6dc6d3026f.png)\n\nThe phase line of a differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the slope field of the equation?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186148224 1074 "From the phase line, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along these lines.\n\n- $f(y)<0$ for $y<-2.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are negative in this region.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ for $-2<y<0$ or $y>0.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are positive in these regions.\n\nThe only slope field that satisfies the above conditions is the following.\n\n![](images/64/64d0e59ba56d8c2a582cc80d267c258681f26badf2b77fddb79c3ff17bdd7249.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147074 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186148225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395140 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148225 1071 "![](images/b9/b958bd4db4353cfe7a852b79f7144d0b48b85c48b3d97f813b09db2858ec110a.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148225 1071 "![](images/b9/b958bd4db4353cfe7a852b79f7144d0b48b85c48b3d97f813b09db2858ec110a.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(y)$, where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186148225 1074 "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-3,y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore $y=-3,0,3$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/66/666ae97e41e825161c68cc6146a06c70b87c6d84b11ae8116c0d415c340987fa.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186147086 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186148227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395146 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148227 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y = 0$ is a source\n- $y = 2$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186148227 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=2$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186148227 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=2$ points upward, the arrow just above it points upward too.\n\n![](images/70/70c6b5e466eb64695ec099bba5ee02fbacf89c60b0eac48ebdb4dbe47735bf14.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/f3/f3789101292914032885458730bfaabf42e75a9447f43a1e7409c15fb8cbbaef.png)\n\n \n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line." 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186152181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394907 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186152182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393953 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186152182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394942 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186152182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394949 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186152291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393771 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394826 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394468 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394234 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394227 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394751 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157304 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394667 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157306 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394660 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157402 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394805 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157402 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394812 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157402 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394819 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157429 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394118 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394111 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394778 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394785 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394084 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394098 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155667 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186157796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394105 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157796 1071 "Find $f^{″}(x, y)$ for the function $f(x, y) = x\\sin y + xy$." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186157796 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157796 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1 & -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155865 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186157829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394462 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157829 1074 "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"the marker is black or it is purple\" with the phrase \"the marker is purple or it is black.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The marker is purple or it is black, or it is black.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"The marker is purple, or it is black or it is black.\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"it is black or it is black\" to just \"it is black.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The marker is purple, or it is black.\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"The marker is purple or black.\"" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155961 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186157845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394565 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157845 1071 "![](images/92/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$ and the line $x = 2\\sqrt{2}$, as shown above. What is\n$∬_(D)\\frac{y}{x}dA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186157845 1071 "![](images/92/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and the line $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157845 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/37/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156015 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186157854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394654 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157854 1071 "![](images/6f/6f7f37b10d483f7ce2bf147f316195b4be578ae62da8538a3aac4a8dbfa2ec1c.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the upper-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2$, as shown above. What is\n$∬_(D)(x - y)dA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186157854 1071 "![](images/6f/6f7f37b10d483f7ce2bf147f316195b4be578ae62da8538a3aac4a8dbfa2ec1c.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the upper-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157854 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186156639 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186157958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394772 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157958 1071 "${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186157958 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx$?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186157958 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186161974 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394853 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186161994 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394962 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186161994 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394969 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186161999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394914 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186161999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394921 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186161999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394928 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186161999 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394935 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162013 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395009 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395044 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395051 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395058 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162021 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395065 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162021 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395072 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162021 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395079 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162023 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395127 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162025 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395092 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395187 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395194 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160916 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186162609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394880 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162609 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162609 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form $x^{T}Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P$." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186162609 1074 "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161042 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186162630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394956 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162630 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162630 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $D = P^{T}AP$, where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P$?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186162630 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161096 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186162639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394983 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162639 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186162639 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x = Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x) = x^{T}Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form $y^{T}Dy$." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186162639 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_