4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186166736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395152 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186166757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394989 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186166758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394996 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186166840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395336 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186166841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395664 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186166842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395678 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186166842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395685 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186166843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395699 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165596 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186167403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395086 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167403 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167403 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, what is the second column of $U$?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186167403 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166172 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186167499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395201 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167499 1071 "The position $r$, in meters, of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $r(t) = ⟨\\sqrt{2}t, t, t^{3}⟩$, where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the tangential component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t = 1s$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186167499 1071 "The position $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{2}t,\\,t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the tangential component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167499 1074 "The tangential component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{T}=\\frac{v\\cdot a}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector and evaluating it at $t=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2},\\,1,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ v(1) & =⟨\\sqrt{2},\\,1,\\,3(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2},\\,1,\\,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector and it evaluating at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,6t⟩ \\\\ a(1) & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,6(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $v(1)\\cdot a(1)$ and $||v(1)||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1)\\cdot a(1) & =⟨\\sqrt{2},\\,1,\\,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =18 \\\\ ||v(1)|| & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the tangential component of the acceleration at $t=1$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{T} & =\\frac{v(1)\\cdot a(1)}{||v(1)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangential component of the acceleration is $3\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166460 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186167547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395576 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167547 1071 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x, y, z) = x^{2}yz + z^{2}$ a potential function?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186167547 1071 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186167547 1074 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k.\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394886 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394893 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394900 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172043 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395383 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172044 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394015 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172044 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395120 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172046 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395471 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172051 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395322 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172052 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395329 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172053 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395492 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172053 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395513 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172055 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395506 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172056 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395520 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171555 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186172660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395295 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172660 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172660 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2rad/s$. Given that the particle is located at $(3, 0)$ when $t = 0$, find the acceleration of the particle at $t = \\frac{π}{6}$ seconds." 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186172660 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{2}\\,i-\\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-6\\,i-6\\sqrt{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171591 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186172666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395343 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172666 1074 "A simple curve is a curve that doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints.\n\nSo, a curve that intersects itself somewhere between its endpoints is not a simple curve. Among the given options, the only that is not a simple curve is the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2cf93e81b1805202737bccc6658bf02602d676dd21df898694155d9c50430bea.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171603 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186172668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395363 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172668 1074 "A curve is closed if its terminal point coincides with its initial point. So, a curve whose terminal point does not coincide with its initial point is not closed. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is not a closed curve is the following:\n\n![](images/fa/faf5eb05d296b2b1155c829f46c2f69a9df8003a3bd1a495be627acfae09afb4.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171627 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186172672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395390 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172672 1074 "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/0b/0baa675c757282d73c238ab1e81ef7ed252945a95098841bbf52b9f558d693c2.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171639 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186172674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395396 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186172674 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:\n\n \n\n![](images/9a/9a001b71c984559199c1457115621dbd111c69da50f741332b2396ce832ede08.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186175934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394125 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186176583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175606 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186176583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394200 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186176583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186176583 1071 "![](images/6c/6c0f74fa4820cd8e6945666ab90e0eafac4cc1ada7578fb5a053f197adf08084.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t = 1$ the particle is located at the position $x = 2$. What is the position of the particle at time $t = 7$?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 false] [17592186176583 1071 "![](images/6c/6c0f74fa4820cd8e6945666ab90e0eafac4cc1ada7578fb5a053f197adf08084.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=7?$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186176583 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(1)=2$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=1$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=2+0=2\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=2.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186179682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394538 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186184165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393834 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186184165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393876 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186184166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393918 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186184182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394174 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186184182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394531 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186184182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394758 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186184196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394050 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186198362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186393757 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186217158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395601 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186217158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395608 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186234504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395289 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393744 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393743 1069 "q-77606" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393743 1071 "![](images/35/3507450dd4d81fb14269ec651cc3f93ef52d615797ac3e6f4f779a0df98debe2.png)\n\nGiven the triangle $△ABC$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the altitude  corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AB}?$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393743 1072 #uuid "813db841-ac50-5fa6-a6b6-270cab223353" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393743 1074 "The height (or altitude)  corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $C$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{AB},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{AB}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below:\n\n![](images/38/389163fbb07fef4fc36fca5dce7cbe86cd4830d810b0210fe47c2b7cedd4b8c8.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393744 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393745 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393744 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393746 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393744 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393747 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393744 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393748 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393744 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393749 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393744 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393744 1077 "selection" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393744 1079 #edb/ref 17592186393745 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393744 1080 #uuid "8715746d-70c4-5d7d-a61a-15d532009b48" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393745 1081 "images/38/389163fbb07fef4fc36fca5dce7cbe86cd4830d810b0210fe47c2b7cedd4b8c8.png" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393745 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393745 1084 #uuid "5975ee7c-248f-52aa-92e6-7307c035348b" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393746 1081 "images/3e/3e2b6cde516dc1737124576ffdc212331e3ad74f728f47aece5911ed04cf7f5a.png" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393746 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393746 1084 #uuid "69276a71-4933-599e-b951-26b490c52cbe" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393747 1081 "images/77/7720d00d73ea3d4b4cf4905cf57ec12a3953caeb135272ec2d5283dad1479efe.png" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393747 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393747 1084 #uuid "df0291cf-6864-5a2c-a3de-e53242dc7ff8" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393748 1081 "images/c1/c1ecb1d567916c708cc6bbbd394517835e86b4105ae045cf5ef9490b181900fa.png" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393748 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393748 1084 #uuid "5358c8ed-0c53-52b2-9dde-eca396e3f641" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393749 1081 "images/5f/5f4e50dfc88fdb9109961e2cef0dc0b1f7968d7b588e2f944450ac2969075bd7.png" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393749 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393749 1084 #uuid "3896dd03-da28-567c-b00f-2916b9f8afe3" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393750 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393751 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393750 1069 "q-77651" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393750 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393750 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,T(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393750 1072 #uuid "cf74894d-e339-586f-8261-369741108e3a" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393750 1074 "The osculating plane passes through the point that is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The unit tangent vector $T(0)$ and the principal normal vector $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as \n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+sT(0)+uN(0),\\,s,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(1,\\,0,\\,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+sT(0)+uN(0) \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393751 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393752 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393751 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393753 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393751 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393754 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393751 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393755 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393751 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393756 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393751 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393751 1077 "selection" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393751 1079 #edb/ref 17592186393755 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393751 1080 #uuid "78aa28f8-2396-55a6-bbc4-e9955564a261" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393752 1081 "R(s,u)=⟨\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393752 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393752 1084 #uuid "9ec9aa97-f0f7-5a10-8249-361b901e466a" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393753 1081 "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1-\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393753 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393753 1084 #uuid "de4add72-0cd4-537b-80b7-0002434bbdf4" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393754 1081 "R(s,u)=⟨1+3s,\\,2s+3u,\\,2s-3u⟩" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393754 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393754 1084 #uuid "250743fd-4960-51b1-885b-3ed93deac3de" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393755 1081 "R(s,u)=⟨1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393755 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393755 1084 #uuid "07171637-65de-5c83-8235-9dc05a85624e" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393756 1081 "R(s,u)=⟨2+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,3+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393756 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393756 1084 #uuid "28f201ce-0d59-5c71-83a0-18e65a037a4c" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393758 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393757 1069 "q-77683" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393757 1071 "Find the second derivative of $f(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,k$ with respect to $t.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393757 1072 #uuid "19d44fb9-0b26-5bc1-af60-efeec2020695" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186393757 1074 "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}t)\\,k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$