gned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -9{w}_{1}-3{w}_{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{w}_{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{7t}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{7t}-3[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394099 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394100 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394099 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394101 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394099 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394102 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394099 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394103 131941