465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394297 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394297 1072 #uuid "62dadad6-bc72-59c3-b335-faab5922669e" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394297 1074 "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394298 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394299 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394298 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394300 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394298 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394301 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394298 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394302 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394298 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394303 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394298 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394298 1077 "selection" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394298 1079 #edb/ref 17592186394301 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394298 1080 #uuid "8055db6f-b8ff-53ba-9dfb-f141126526ea" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394299 1081 "[\\begi