First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬(¬X⇔¬Y)≡¬[(¬X⇒¬Y)∧(¬Y⇒¬X)]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(¬X⇒¬Y)∧(¬Y⇒¬X)]≡¬[(¬¬X∨¬Y)∧(¬¬Y∨¬X)]\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(¬¬X∨¬Y)∧(¬¬Y∨¬X)]≡¬[(X∨¬Y)∧(Y∨¬X)]\n$$\n\nNext, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(X∨¬Y)∧(Y∨¬X)] & ≡¬(X∨¬Y)∨¬(Y∨¬X) \\\\  & ≡(¬X∧¬¬Y)∨(¬Y∧¬¬X)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we again simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n(¬X∧¬¬Y)∨(¬Y∧¬¬X)≡(¬X∧Y)∨(¬Y∧X)\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394484 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394486 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394483 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394488 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394483 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394483 1077 "selection" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394483 1079 #edb/ref 17592186394487 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394483 1080 #uuid "f1c55f37-dfa2-5f45-acd3-4ca2d5a301de" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394484 1081 "¬(X∨Y)∧X" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394484 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394484 1084 #uuid "c8e0c462-bed9-534d-a8c4-881399656916" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394485 1081 "(¬X∧Y)⇒(¬Y∧X)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394485 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394485 1084 #uuid "b4ba4e7d-b859-5bc6-9152-a638404dd6cd" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394486 1081 "X⇔Y" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394486 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394486 1084 #uuid "9a50d2ad-fe71-5ee1-9903-848ed5950674" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394487 1081 "(¬X∧Y)∨(¬Y∧X)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394487 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394487 1084 #uuid "b8f73c55-ed3b-50d8-b759-f68ddf443173" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394488 1081 "(X∨¬Y)∧(Y∨¬X)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394488 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394488 1084 #uuid "73b69ceb-20b1-5829-8db9-c8f84923f153" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394490 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394489 1069 "q-83228" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394489 1071 "$¬(A⇔¬B)≡$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394489 1072 #uuid "1d70dc23-0b2b-583b-a868-4b96eea33478" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394489 1074 "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬(A⇔¬B)≡¬[(A⇒¬B)∧(¬B⇒A)]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(A⇒¬B)∧(¬B⇒A)]≡¬[(¬A∨¬B)∧(¬¬B∨A)]\n$$\n\nNext, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(¬A∨¬B)∧(¬¬B∨A)] & ≡¬(¬A∨¬B)∨¬(¬¬B∨A) \\\\  & ≡(¬¬A∧¬¬B)∨(¬¬¬B∧¬A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n(¬¬A∧¬¬B)∨(¬¬¬B∧¬A)≡(A∧B)∨(¬B∧¬A)\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394491 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394492 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394493 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394494 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394495 131941