etric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$ Computing $A-(-7)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+7I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $-4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394888 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394889 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394890 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186394887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394891 1