]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395099 1072 #uuid "5112a112-d520-578f-a615-69da3e2d4aa0" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395099 1074 "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 3=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395100 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395101 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395100 107