es/07/07e822b78317e1bc18b5b7060ac867a200b60403ffe0b91eadad850efe865d55.png" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395401 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395401 1084 #uuid "04c399f1-df31-56df-9b13-7e3645c989ed" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395403 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395402 1069 "q-98040" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395402 1071 "![](images/58/58369eb9878aba12bf52d22a930ec348e7b6cf31e1b2c8d77823de1aab5d9c87.png)\n\nThe slope field of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line of the given equation?" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395402 1072 #uuid "4b7cd7a6-7b0b-599b-8ee5-35ee0fead25b" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395402 1074 "From the slope field, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $y=-2$ and $y=0$ are equilibrium solutions.\n\n- For all $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y)<0$ in this region.\n\n- For all $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,0),$ the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y)>0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/fc/fcc9f998111493167284cf5844645ac2201a230b073a9336604fb790ba16bad4.png)" 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395403 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395404 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395403 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395405 13194139533465 17592186045585 true] [17592186395403 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395406 13194139533465 1759218